#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:5
#: af66fbe75cb54799abb9b888364845d0
msgid "Four Channel Planes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:7
#: 49338d3976ef47c69a2868ef5bcc83ec
msgid "The most common type of fixed wing plane is a 4 channel plane. As the name implies, it has 4 output channels, and they control the roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle independently."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:11
#: b04d736a3c834aba9a332743ab496e70
msgid "These are the default outputs for ArduPlane as it is such a common setup. It is commonly referred to as an AETR setup."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:14
#: 55be9a761bdc4d5c80205ac9c81d6161
msgid "Remove the propeller from your aircraft before starting your setup."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:18
#: 265689bf6bfb4a79951fd63865d620a3
msgid "Setting Up Your Plane"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:20
#: a04036e4ce0249ee9de7f9e67a271712
msgid "The most important step to setting up the plane is having the correct inputs, outputs, and reversals. Inputs are covered on the :ref:`RC input setup page <rc-throw-trim>`. After the RC inputs are configured, configure the outputs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:24
#: 832520dc6f9b4d5c92d97fbc1d1ecbb5
msgid "Make sure the :ref:`AHRS_ORIENTATION<AHRS_ORIENTATION>` is set correctly for the autopilot. If it is incorrect, this setup will fail, and the plane may crash upon entry into any stabilize mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:27
#: 88f4d54469f14766bdac89e51838eb5c
msgid "Servo cables can be connected to any output of the autopilot, but using the default channels 1-4 listed below is recommended. Set the SERVOn_FUNCTION to the appropriate values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:31
#: 5c02663e5cd04314bf83a70f234c7362
msgid "You can do this by setting the  parameter directly using Ground Station software such as Mission Planner or QGroundControl using either the full parameter lists or setup tab for outputs. In Mission Planner you can do this on the SETUP -> Mandatory Hardware -> Servo Output page. Each channel has a dropdown which can be used to select the function of the channel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:43
#: 2bdf3c8a36814074b99e128cf47597a3
msgid "Most 4-channel aircraft utilize a Y-splitter for ailerons. It works well if the servos have equal travel ranges and mirrored movement. If you wish to set the trim, max, or min values for each aileron servo independently, then use another output like channel 5 for the second aileron. Be sure to set that channel's function correctly (4). This principle applies to any additional servo or motor output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:51
#: 45ecb7cf7bdf408faef4571885a889a9
msgid "Servo Function & Reversal"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:53
#: 6aa69fbcd10744178dc28534230a62fd
msgid "The next step is to correct the servo reversals. Connect the battery (with propeller removed) and turn on the RC transmitter. Switch to FBWA mode using the function switch or a ground station command, and disable the safety switch (if fitted). We use FBWA for setup because it prevents double-reversing inputs and outputs. Double-reversing causes correct manual behavior, but dangerous and destabilizing behavior in other flight modes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:60
#: 955d399cb05540f69d2af670648dec36
msgid "When the plane is level, the servos should be near their trim (neutral) values. Move the plane and leave the transmitter sticks centered while monitoring the control surfaces to determine if the reversals are correct. See the table for the correct control surface response to the movements. In each instance, the plane should move its control surfaces to level itself and coordinate its turns."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:79
#: e3325ba3207845ca8c0aee5569fab47b
msgid "If the any of the control surfaces do not respond correctly, reverse the output by changing the corresponding SERVOn_REVERSED setting (from 0 to 1, or from 1 to 0)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:83
#: 979a8ca44abd482f9a34f2a5d319777a
msgid "The output can be reversed in Mission Planner on the SETUP/Mandatory Hardware/Servo Output page, just check the box for the channel that needs to be reversed. This will set the output's SERVOn_REVERSED parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:85
#: 662c9fe0885840869ff934e788410377
msgid ":ref:`KFF_RDDRMIX<KFF_RDDRMIX>` mut not be set to 0 (the default value is 0.5) for checking rudder movement in FBWA as the plane is rolled away from level. If no automatic coupling of rudder to aileron for coordinated turns by the autopilot is desired, then reset it to zero after this setup check."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:88
#: 1eeb80416341403bbad9e83581458957
msgid "Confirm RC Transmitter Input"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:90
#: 33a30d60eda1473696565eadfe70a720
msgid "Keep the plane level in FBWA mode and command the following inputs by moving the sticks on your transmitter:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:104
#: 6aa0bc1b7ae14806a5a57da9e47e419a
msgid "If the control surfaces do not respond correctly, change the RCn_reversed parameter (from 0 to 1, or from 1 to 0). Do NOT reverse the output on your transmitter. It must be changed in the autopilot! Double check MANUAL mode for the same inputs. If everything is setup correctly, the plane should be almost ready to fly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:110
#: 26b51383cc984b2b941d68147910d1f4
msgid "If in MANUAL mode the surfaces move backward with stick movements now, the corresponding RC input should be reversed. To do this, in Mission Planner RCn_REVERSED can be easily set on the SETUP/Mandatory Hardware/Radio Calibration page. There is a check box \"Reverse\" next to each input bar. You can reverse the correct parameter by checking the box. However, if :ref:`common-radio-control-calibration` was followed correctly, changing RC input reversal will not be necessary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:113
#: 6876da07143d4a7b81df6dadb1a874cf
msgid "Servo Trim"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:115
#: 2f6e02324ffa433c8ef48ffa12f8bb31
msgid "Switch to MANUAL mode in order to adjust the servo trim values. The servo trim is in the SERVOn_TRIM parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:118
#: 9236c347df2843528b11e517315b01e7
msgid "Adjust the trim values so that the servo is centered when the transmitter sticks are centered. If the trim value is not between 1450 and 1550 PWM, mechanical trim adjustment is recommended."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:123
#: 25462f4acd4140a8a701732a4c68849b
msgid "Servo Throw"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:125
#: e2beac3a184e40429aa1c32e20a8130a
msgid "Finally adjust the servo throws (range of movement for each of the servos). This should also be done in MANUAL mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:128
#: bd68738b93ad4fe4894ef1622bd1efd5
msgid "Check any instructions that came with the plane for suggested throw values. These are often specified in millimeters or inches of movement of the trailing edge of the control surface close to the fuselage. If suggested throw values are not found, then choose a throw that doesn't cause the servos to \"bind\" (often indicated by a high pitched sound when servos stall)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-four-channel-plane.rst:135
#: 04b61b59e0c24a6eba25fc6ee5253b33
msgid "To adjust the throw, change the SERVOn_MIN and SERVOn_MAX values. The defaults are 1100 to 1900. On many aircraft, more throw may be desired. Changing throws to 1000 to 2000 or beyond is normal. Make sure that the servos are still moving when nearing the extreme values."
msgstr ""

